<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alyattes</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 610–560 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> · Also known as Alyattes of Lydia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alyattes was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydia">Lydia</a> from around 610 to 560 BCE, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mermnad-dynasty">Mermnad dynasty</a>, and father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croesus">Croesus</a>. He expelled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimmerians">Cimmerians</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, destroyed Smyrna, and fought a long war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/median-empire">Median Empire</a> that ended after the solar eclipse of 585 BCE with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kizilirmak">Halys</a> river as the border. His huge burial mound at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bin-tepe">Bin Tepe</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a> survives.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alyattes.jpg" alt="Alyattes" width="320" height="133" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG Coins · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:KINGS_of_LYDIA._Alyattes._Circa_620-10-564-53_BC.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Alyattes was the son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadyattes">Sadyattes</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydia">Lydia</a> and Sadyattes's sister and queen, Lyde, both children of King Ardys. His great-grandfather Gyges had repelled attacks by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimmerians">Cimmerians</a>, nomads from the Eurasian Steppe, but in 644 BCE they defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydians">Lydians</a>, sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a> and killed Gyges. The Thracian Treres, allied with the Cimmerians and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lycians">Lycians</a>, sacked Sardis again, except for its citadel. Alyattes came to the throne in the midst of this turmoil. His name is Lydian in origin and meant the state of being a lion.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>He began by continuing his father's war with the Ionian city of Miletus, a series of raids to seize the Milesians' grain. It ended in a treaty of friendship and alliance with the tyrant Thrasybulus, with Lydian metal exchanged for Milesian cereal. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> tells that Alyattes fell ill after Lydian troops burned a temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a> and recovered only after rebuilding it, an account that appears not to be factual. He also dedicated a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> crater and an iron stand to Delphi. Relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a>, tied to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mermnad-dynasty">Mermnads</a> by marriage, were always good.</p><p>With the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carians">Carians</a> he kept the alliances begun under Gyges, and with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madyes">Madyes</a> he defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimmerians">Cimmerians</a>. He extended Lydian rule east into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrygia">Phrygia</a>. Around 600 BCE he turned on Smyrna, which gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydia">Lydia</a> a port; he then failed against Clazomenae, whose people were helped by the cavalry of Colophon. He later had the Colophonian cavalry massacred at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a> and placed Colophon under direct Lydian rule. His war with the Median king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyaxares">Cyaxares</a> began in 590 BCE over Scythian refugees at Sardis and lasted five years, until a solar eclipse in 585 BCE during a battle was taken by both sides as an omen to end it. Alyattes's daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aryenis">Aryenis</a> married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astyages">Astyages</a>, son of Cyaxares, to seal the peace.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Alyattes died soon after the Battle of the Eclipse. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croesus">Croesus</a>, born of a Carian noblewoman, prevailed in a struggle for the throne against Pantaleon, whose mother was Greek. He is credited with the first coins, struck in electrum, and with standardising their weight. His tomb, a mound about sixty metres high and two hundred and fifty metres across at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bin-tepe">Bin Tepe</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a>, covers a vault of marble blocks. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> describes it as the work of market men, artisans and prostitutes. It was excavated in 1853 by Ludwig Peter Spiegelthal, by which time it had been looted.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alyattes” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Herodotus credits Glaucus of Chios with inventing the welding of iron; he made a famous iron stand for a silver bowl dedicated at Delphi by <b>Alyattes of Lydia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chios">Chios · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Lydia produced the earliest known coins in the late seventh century BCE: lumps of electrum of standard weight stamped with a lion’s head, probably under <b>Alyattes</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-lydia">Kingdom of Lydia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Power was reinforced through ritual, elite networks and marriage alliances, among them the marriage of Astyages to the daughter of the Lydian king <b>Alyattes</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/median-empire">Median Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Herodotus relates that under Sadyattes and then <b>Alyattes</b> the Lydians invaded Milesian territory every year for eleven years, burning the crops, but could not take the city because Miletus controlled the sea; the tyrant Thrasybulus finally persuaded Alyattes to make peace by staging a show of plenty when…</blockquote><a href="/empire/miletus">Miletus · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Around c. 635 BCE the Scythians under Madyes and the Lydians under <b>Alyattes</b> defeated them for good.</blockquote><a href="/culture/cimmerians">Cimmerians · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Their leader Dugdamme, the Lygdamis of Greek sources, died campaigning against Assyria around 640 BCE, and <b>Alyattes of Lydia</b> finally drove the remaining bands out of Anatolia.</blockquote><a href="/state/cimmerians">Cimmerians</a></li></ul></section>
